Z||000000||fiero56||08-12-2001||11:31 PM||||First off, I would like to thank Scott(87ECO5) for the use of his tools, driveway, assistance in replacing my rear calipers, and for helping until 11pm. It was a two man job, and I'm only one person. We got my rear calipers installed, got one SS brake line installed, and got the system bled. I now have brakes that would pass an inspection(if we had inspections here), and a working parking brake. My fingers are still "pruned" from all that brake fluid.<P>I rounded off the nut that attaches the hard-line to the flexible caliper hose. When I am finally able to get this nut off, can I cut the line before the nut, install a new nut, and flare the end to match factory? Would take off about 3/4" of hard-line. I don't see a problem, but I have overlooked things before.||||reg||5
Z||000001||BOILERMAKER||08-13-2001||08:17 AM||||The only problem I see is that you may not be able to use the flange connection that holds the caliper line to the body of the car.  Other than that, I would say go for it and drill another hole so that the flange still has a place to secure the line to.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||08-13-2001||10:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>First off, I would like to thank Scott(87ECO5)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are very welcome, Drew!  Glad I could help.<P>Let me know what you find out about that fitting.||||reg||14
